' --- manual: everything below this line is hand-authored; the script preserves it ---

' The automated pass reads the public headers under service/include/, so the
' six types it draws are the process shell. Everything the service actually
' does -- the feed control plane, the event registrars and the feed controller
' -- lives in private headers under src/ that the pass never reads, and the
' shell's own boxes arrive empty because their content is methods.
'
' Redeclaring a class with its fully-qualified name merges the members into
' the box the automated pass already drew. No namespace block is reopened:
' this PlantUML version crashes when a namespace that already holds classes is
' declared a second time.

class ores::synthetic::service::app::application {
    +application()
    +run(io_ctx : io_context&, cfg : config::options) : awaitable<void>
    {static} -make_context(db_opts : database_options) : database::context
}
note right of "ores::synthetic::service::app::application"
The composition root, and the whole of the service's startup
order: open NATS, build the authenticated service-to-service
client, make the DB context, start the entity-change event
pipeline, ensure the two JetStream streams, construct the
feed_controller, run the boot-time auto-start walk, then hand
the handler-registration lambdas to ores.service's run()
before shutting the controller and the event source down.
end note

class ores::synthetic::service::app::host {
    {static} +execute(args : vector<string>, std_output : ostream&, error_output : ostream&, io_ctx : io_context&) : awaitable<int>
}
note right of "ores::synthetic::service::app::host"
The console workflow. The automated pass drew this box empty
because every member is a static method.
end note

class ores::synthetic::service::config::parser {
    +parse(arguments : vector<string>, info : ostream&, error : ostream&) : optional<options>
}
note right of "ores::synthetic::service::config::parser"
Returns an empty optional rather than throwing when the
command line asks for help or fails to parse, so the caller
decides the exit code. Logging is unavailable here by
design: the logger starts only after the parse succeeds, so
a parse error is reported on the error stream instead.
end note

class ores::synthetic::service::messaging::event_registrar {
    {static} +register_event_mappings(event_source : postgres_event_source&, event_bus : event_bus&, nats : nats::client&) : vector<subscription>
}
note right of "ores::synthetic::service::messaging::event_registrar"
The one hand-maintained line-per-entity call site in the
component. It composes the nine generated per-entity
registrars and takes ownership of the subscriptions they
return, so they outlive the call.
end note

' The nine generated per-entity event registrars. A free function cannot be
' drawn as a class, so each is redeclared here; together they are what the
' event_registrar above composes.

class ores::synthetic::service::messaging::register_folder_event_mapping {
    {static} +register_folder_event_mapping(event_source : postgres_event_source&, event_bus : event_bus&, nats : nats::client&) : subscription
}
note top of "ores::synthetic::service::messaging::register_folder_event_mapping"
Free function, one per entity, and the reason the box here
is hand-drawn: the automated pass reads types, not free
functions. Each registers the entity's Postgres NOTIFY
channel on the event source and subscribes the event bus,
so a committed row change becomes a NATS publish. The other
eight are named register_<entity>_event_mapping and carry
the same three arguments.
end note
note bottom of "ores::synthetic::service::messaging::register_folder_event_mapping"
The nine channels, one per synthetic table:
ores_synthetic_folders, _fx_spot_generation_configs,
_gmm_components, _ir_curve_generation_configs,
_ir_curve_generation_config_process_parameter_values,
_ir_curve_template_entries, _market_data_generation_configs,
_yield_curve_process_parameter_definitions and
_yield_curve_process_types. A row change on any of them
reaches NATS; no other synthetic table publishes an event.
end note

' The feed control plane: the controller, its handlers, and the private
' handlers the service registrars subscribe.

class ores::synthetic::service::feed_kind_registry {
    +feed_kind_registry()
    +feed_kind_registry(d : deps)
    +register_kind(k : feed_kind)
    +factory() : feed_factory&
    +find(kind : string_view) : feed_kind*
    +all() : vector<feed_kind*>
    +permits_all_configs(ctx : database::context&) : bool
    +rows(ctx : database::context&) : vector<feed_kind_row>
    +targets(ctx : database::context&) : vector<feed_start_target>
    +resolve(ctx : database::context&, feed_config_id : string&) : optional<feed_start_target>
    +folder_subtree(ctx : database::context&, root_id : uuid&) : set<uuid>
    +make_feed(target : feed_start_target&, bctx : feed_build_context&) : feed_start_attempt
    +reply_unknown_kind(nats : nats::client&, msg : nats::message&, kind : string&)
    +vintage_validity(ctx : database::context&, auth_nats : nats_client&, caller_bearer_token : string&) : vector<vintage_validity_entry>
    -make_target(k : feed_kind&, c : feed_kind_candidate, containers_by_id : map<uuid,market_data_generation_config>*) : optional<feed_start_target>
    -check_vintage(row : feed_kind_row&, auth_nats : nats_client&, caller_bearer_token : string&) : vintage_validity_entry
}
note right of "ores::synthetic::service::feed_kind_registry"
The one per-kind registry: the config-plane's dispatch table.
A feed_kind carries the factory kind, the config permission, a
candidates closure that loads that kind's config rows, and the
two closures that decode and reply one simulate request. The
registry owns the reads that are not per-kind -- the
market_data_generation_config container lookup and the folder
subtree walk -- and the bodies of every verb that used to be
written once per asset class: the startability gate and the
producer construction (make_feed), the permission gate
(permits_all_configs), the vintage check and the kind-neutral
simulate envelope (run_simulate_paths). Injecting the three
non-per-kind reads is what keeps every handler free of an
ores.synthetic.core repository, and what lets a stub kind be
driven with no database.
end note

class ores::synthetic::service::feed_controller {
    +feed_controller(nats : nats::client&, auth_nats : nats_client&, bindings : shared_ptr<feed_binding_store>)
    +~feed_controller()
    +start(feed : shared_ptr<IFeed>, binding_mode : binding_mode, caller_bearer_token : string) : start_result
    +add(feed : shared_ptr<IFeed>, binding_mode : binding_mode, caller_bearer_token : string, out_conflicting_source_name : string*) : bool
    +stop(key : string) : size_t
    +shutdown()
    +running_count() : size_t
    +list(kind : string) : vector<string>
    +running_source_name_for_conflict_key(conflict_key : string) : optional<string>
    -bind_feed(source_name : string, binding_mode : binding_mode, caller_bearer_token : string)
    -find_conflict(feed : IFeed&, excluding_source_name : string) : optional<string>
    -start_running(feed : shared_ptr<IFeed>, binding_mode : binding_mode)
    -status_loop()
    -log_status()
}
note right of "ores::synthetic::service::feed_controller"
One controller serves every asset class: feeds_ is keyed by
source_name, and every feed enters through the IFeed
interface, so the per-kind producers differ only in what
they publish. At most one feed runs per (qualifier, role)
pair -- feeds_conflict -- and a second one is refused by
name, never by stopping the first.
end note
note bottom of "ores::synthetic::service::feed_controller"
Two ways in, one binding gate. add() is the config-driven
path (boot auto-start, the folder cascade); start(IFeed) is
the on-demand path and distinguishes already_running from
qualifier_conflict. Both call bind_feed after releasing the
mutex, so a bound feed creates or confirms its marketdata
feed_binding and a sandboxed feed never does, for every
asset class; the marketdata round-trip sits behind
feed_binding_store. A re-start of an already-running feed
keeps the mode it is running with and still confirms its
binding. An internal start with no end-user session has no
party to bind under and skips the bind. One tick thread per
feed, plus one status thread that logs publish counts every
minute.
end note

class ores::synthetic::service::feed_binding_store {
    +save_if_absent(source_name : string, caller_bearer_token : string) : expected<void,string>
}
note right of "ores::synthetic::service::feed_binding_store"
The marketdata round-trip seam a feed_controller binds through. The controller
owns the one rule, a bound feed is bound and a sandboxed feed is not; the
store owns the list-then-write over NATS, so the rule is testable without a
live marketdata service. Idempotent: an already-bound source keeps its one row.
end note

class ores::synthetic::service::feed_config_handler {
    +feed_config_handler(nats : nats::client&, auth_nats : nats_client&, ctrl : shared_ptr<feed_controller>, ctx : database::context, verifier : optional<jwt_authenticator>, registry : feed_kind_registry&)
    +start(msg : nats::message)
    +stop(msg : nats::message)
    +list(msg : nats::message)
    -reply_start_outcome(msg : nats::message&, resp : start_feed_response&, feed : shared_ptr<IFeed>, binding_mode : binding_mode, bearer : string)
    -authenticated_context(verb : string_view, msg : nats::message&) : optional<database::context>
}
note right of "ores::synthetic::service::feed_config_handler"
Serves synthetic.v1.ops.start_feed / .stop / .list --
one handler for every asset class, replacing the per-kind
handlers that came before it. The client sends a config_id
and nothing else: the registry resolves it against every
registered kind and reports which kind claimed it, so the
handler names no config table and no permission string.
Missing, disabled and vintage-missing all answer with one
message shape regardless of kind. start() asks the registry
for the producer (make_feed applies the gate, loads the
children and builds through the factory) and hands it to the
controller. stop() is idempotent: zero stopped means it was
already stopped.
end note

class ores::synthetic::service::folder_feed_control_handler {
    +folder_feed_control_handler(nats : nats::client&, ctrl : shared_ptr<feed_controller>, auth_nats : nats_client&, ctx : database::context, verifier : optional<jwt_authenticator>, registry : feed_kind_registry&)
    +start(msg : nats::message)
    +stop(msg : nats::message)
    -parse_folder_id(s : string&, out : uuid&) : bool
    -authenticated_context(verb : string_view, msg : nats::message&) : optional<database::context>
}
note right of "ores::synthetic::service::folder_feed_control_handler"
Serves marketdata.v1.ops.start_feeds_under_folder and
.stop_folder. The single place that turns "start everything
under this folder" into a sequence of producer starts: it
resolves the folder subtree through the registry, then fans
out over every row the registry holds, of every asset class,
through the same factory and controller -- so Qt, ores.shell
and a wt workflow step all get one behaviour from one
request. There is one loop, not one per kind: by_kind and
stopped_by_kind are pre-populated from the registrations.
The folder and feed rows are tenant and party scoped under
RLS, so a valid JWT is required and the queries run in the
caller's own context.
end note

class ores::synthetic::service::vintage_validity_handler {
    +vintage_validity_handler(nats : nats::client&, auth_nats : nats_client&, ctx : database::context, verifier : optional<jwt_authenticator>, registry : feed_kind_registry&)
    +list(msg : nats::message)
}
note right of "ores::synthetic::service::vintage_validity_handler"
Serves marketdata.v1.ops.get_vintage_validity.
It asks the registry for one validity entry per config row of
every registered kind, and the registry's one check reads the
anchor coordinate the kind itself supplies -- the ORE key's
own spot datum for FX, the shortest-tenor DEPOSIT pillar of
the config's own Curve Template for IR. There is no stored
column and no trigger, so the answer is correct even straight
after an import that added observation rows and no feed rows.
A feed whose price_source is not "vintage" comes back
applicable=false and is not checked.
end note

class ores::synthetic::service::simulate_handler {
    +simulate_handler(nats : nats::client&, ctx : database::context, verifier : optional<jwt_authenticator>, registry : feed_kind_registry&)
    +simulate(msg : nats::message, kind : string&)
}
note right of "ores::synthetic::service::simulate_handler"
Serves one simulate subject per registered kind. The handler
holds no protocol type, no process type and no kind literal:
the registry's registration supplies the subject, the config
permission, the decode closure that turns the kind's own
request into the shared envelope, and the reply closure that
answers with the kind's own typed response.
run_simulate_paths owns the clamp, the per-path seeding and
the stepping. Nothing is persisted and nothing is published:
it is a dry run so a caller can see the configured behaviour
before starting a feed.
end note

class ores::synthetic::service::ir_curve_preview_handler {
    +ir_curve_preview_handler(nats : nats::client&, ctx : database::context, verifier : optional<jwt_authenticator>, registry : feed_kind_registry&)
    +preview_shape(msg : nats::message, kind : string&)
}
note right of "ores::synthetic::service::ir_curve_preview_handler"
The curve-shape dry run. preview_shape has no FX equivalent,
because FX has one scalar spot where a curve has a tenor
grid: it resolves the request's Curve Template entries
through the shared resolver and prices each one at one
process state, so the editor can draw the shape those entries
imply. Stateless -- nothing is persisted or published -- and
clamped to the protocol's own maxima. Its permission comes
from the registry's registration for the kind the registrar
names, so the handler holds no permission literal.
end note

class ores::synthetic::service::registrar {
    {static} +register_handlers(nats : nats::client&, auth_nats : nats_client&, ctrl : shared_ptr<feed_controller>, ctx : database::context, verifier : optional<jwt_authenticator>, registry : feed_kind_registry&) : vector<subscription>
}
note right of "ores::synthetic::service::registrar"
The control-plane registrar, distinct from
ores.synthetic.core's entity registrar. It subscribes
synthetic.v1.ops.start_feed/.stop/.list,
one simulate subject per registered kind (the registry's own
registration names the subject and the kind string),
synthetic.v1.ops.preview_ir_curve_shape -- IR only, and the
one explicit subscription the registrar keeps, because that
verb has no second registration to iterate --
marketdata.v1.ops.start_feeds_under_folder/.stop_folder and
marketdata.v1.ops.get_vintage_validity. All on the
ores.synthetic.service queue, and each lambda constructs its
handler per message.
end note

